<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imperialism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1870 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imperialism is the maintaining and extending of power over foreign nations. Named in the 19th century, it drove the European empires, joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, to conquer and rule much of the world, justified by a civilizing mission and theories of race and enforced with great violence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in the colonial era: it produced remarkable institutional resilience, so that the Golden Stool was never surrendered and the Asante kingship survives as a recognised traditional authority within Ghana today, but it also limited the empire's capacity to concentrate the resources needed for total mobilisation against British <b>imperialism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…was marked by a pragmatic diversity that contrasted with the more centralised administrative systems of French or Spanish <b>imperialism</b>: territories were held under very different constitutional arrangements, from the self-governing dominions of Canada (1867), Australia (1901), New Zealand and South Africa (1910) — which had elected parliaments and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…the campaign, including Mark Twain with King Leopold’s Soliloquy (1905) and Arthur Conan Doyle with The Crime of the Congo (1909), while Joseph Conrad’s Heart of Darkness, drawing on his own journey up the river in 1890, became one of the most widely read works on European <b>imperialism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Nevertheless, Italian <b>imperialism</b> remains deeply contested, marked by the internment of much of Cyrenaica's population in camps and the execution of the resistance leader Omar Mukhtar in 1931, and by the massacre in Addis Ababa that followed the attempt on Viceroy Rodolfo Graziani's life in February 1937.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Rattanakosin Siam was the only state in mainland or maritime Southeast Asia to keep its independence through the age of European <b>imperialism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The occupation of 1912–1933 and Sandino’s resistance made Nicaragua a symbol of anti-<b>imperialism</b> throughout Latin America, and the revolutionaries of the Sandinista National Liberation Front (FSLN), founded in 1961, took his name.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
